{
  "_recorded": "PMS 1.43.3 GET /hubs, 2026-08-18, fetched with a SHARED account's own server token on a server carrying 46 per-person rows.",
  "_sanitized": [
    "The real response had 33 hubs (25 collection hubs, 8 home hubs); this keeps 9 collection hubs and 2 home hubs — enough for every case below, not the whole library.",
    "Each hub's Metadata array is real in STRUCTURE and reduced to 2 items from 6, with `size` and `hubKey` reduced to match so the file stays internally consistent. Item fields are a subset: the real items carry ~30 keys each (Media, Genre, Role, guid, thumb, summary...).",
    "Titles are replaced with neutral ones and the server name with 'Example Server'. The two 'Because you watched X' rows name real titles a real person watched, which is watch history and does not belong in a public repo; the zero-width marker suffixes are kept verbatim because the tests read them.",
    "ratingKeys, hub `key`/`hubIdentifier`/`context`/`type`/`style`/`promoted`, and the Metadata item TYPES are verbatim."
  ],
  "_what_it_proves": [
    "A per-person row appears on Home as its OWN hub, not as a Metadata item inside one — the row's identity lives in `key` (/library/collections/<id>/children), which is what `_HUB_COLLECTION_KEY` matches. Every hub here carries Metadata and not one of those items is of type 'collection', so a detector that scanned Metadata for collections would find nothing on a real server.",
    "A real PMS DOES apply a share `label!=` filter to /hubs. This account's filter carries Shortlist's exclusions and its Home shows its own four rows plus the shared row — and not one of the other 45 people's rows. Previously assumed; measured here.",
    "Home also carries hubs that are not collections at all (Continue Watching, Recently Added). They must never be mistaken for a row.",
    "Rows from other tools (Kometa) sit in the same response and must be left alone — plex-safety rule 4 coexistence, in the wild."
  ],
